. First understand the flow
Suppose you want to insert an employee into SQL Server.
var employee = new Employee
{
    Name = "Saurabh",
    Salary = 50000
};

db.Employees.Add(employee);

db.SaveChanges();


What happens?
- Add() → Tells EF Core to track this employee as a new record.
- SaveChanges() → Executes the INSERT query in SQL Server and saves the record permanently.
Important: Add() does not directly insert the record into the database.
2. Difference between these methods
Method	Meaning
Add()	Marks entity for insertion; synchronous API
AddAsync()	Marks entity for insertion; asynchronous API, mainly useful for special async value generators
SaveChanges()	Saves tracked changes synchronously
SaveChangesAsync()	Saves tracked changes asynchronously

Example in .NET Core
public async Task AddEmployee()
{
    var employee = new Employee
    {
        Name = "Saurabh",
        Salary = 50000
    };

    await db.Employees.AddAsync(employee);

    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
}


Interview tip: SaveChangesAsync() is generally preferred in asynchronous Web API methods because it avoids blocking the thread while database I/O is in progress.
